  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nShowers and thunderstorms firing up early this afternoon with \nthe help of a weak mid/upper-level short wave trough moving \nthrough the area, and perhaps a subtle inverted surface trough,\nwill continue becoming more numerous amid the afternoon heating\nand destabilization. With rich moisture content, evinced by PW \nvalues of 1.5 to 2.0 inches, and somewhat slow motion, localized\nflash flooding is possible.\n\nThe short wave was one of a series along the southern edge of a\nlong wave trough that kind of angles from central Canada south-\nsoutheastward to the Ohio Valley early this afternoon. While \nthunderstorms may tend to wane in intensity and coverage after \nsunset this evening, additional short waves/convective \ncomplexes keep the thunderstorm threat going through tonight and\nSaturday, with a diurnal uptick in coverage and intensity again\nSaturday afternoon.\n\nRiver valley fog is possible overnight tonight into Saturday \nmorning, most likely, and most persistent Saturday morning \nwhere thunderstorms occur this afternoon or evening.\n\nThe eastern U.S. trough position moves to or just off the east \ncoast early next week, allowing a west to northwest mid/upper-\nlevel flow to take hold over the forecast area. After a brief \nreprieve in response to this Sunday into Monday as weak surface\nhigh pressure become established, and PW values drop into the \n1.0-1.5 inch range, the Ad Nauseam of daily thunderstorms \nreturns Monday afternoon or evening, as the surface high gives \nway to low level southwest flow beneath the mid/upper-level \nwest to northwest flow increasing PW values back up into the \n1.5 to 2.0 inch range.\n\nWhile localized flooding will again be a threat given relatively\nslow storm motion and high PW values, increased shear associated\nwith the aforementioned flow pattern at times may increase the \nsevere weather threat in the form of a damaging wind potential \nearly next week.\n\nThe Weather Prediction Center has the forecast area in a \nMarginal Risk for Excessive Rainfall through Saturday night,\nthen after the break on Sunday, they place northern portions of\nthe forecast area in a Marginal Risk Monday, and then throughout\nthe forecast area next Tuesday, with a Slight Risk area nudging\nin from the west along the Ohio RIver. The orientation shift to\nwest-northwest to east-southeast early in the next work week \nreflects the west-northwest mid/upper level flow.\n\nIn a large expansion from the Middle Atlantic to the south-\ncentral Plains in the midday update, the Storm Prediction \nCenter has the forecast area in a Marginal Risk for Severe\nThunderstorms this afternoon into this evening, and then just\ngeneral thunderstorm for the weekend. The Storm Prediction \nCenter has introduced a 15% Severe Thunderstorm Risk in a west \nto east orientation north and northwest of the forecast area, \nfor Monday, and allude to a damaging wind potential associated \nwith a west to east front and west-noprthwest mid-level flow \nthrough Day 8, next Saturday.",
